#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172995} :activity/id #uuid "1249b882-9023-5a8e-b9f3-da0aef8069d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171895 :question/id #uuid "f6f11512-c37f-5922-b642-5290003b1d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-9492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050779}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405029}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "ba2fdaeb-e9c0-578e-a23e-d08f48959fe8" :step/math-academy-id 35542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171896 :question/id #uuid "6753365e-9915-5be9-9a6d-d4c40b2caea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5777905c-e581-5454-bdbe-29878ad1237b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387395}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a0e6a5fd-e87e-5316-9084-5168c4efa1da" :step/math-academy-id 35543 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173201 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a path $C$ in the $xy$-plane, the arc length $L$ of $C$ can be computed as the line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int_C \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is defined parametrically as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle, \\quad a\\leq t \\leq b ,\n$$\n\nthen we can also calculate the arc length of $C$ as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{a}^{b}||\\mathbf r'(t)||\\,\\textrm d t = \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L,$ we say that the **arc-length element** $\\textrm ds$ for a parametrically defined curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, we can think of $\\textrm ds$ as representing the change in arc length that arises due to a vanishingly small change in $t.$ By summing (integrating) all of these small changes, we arrive at the arc length of $C.$ We'll explore this in more detail at the end of the lesson.\n\nThe idea of an arc length element is useful when we want to think about line integrals in different coordinate systems. It also illustrates a fundamental relationship between the arc length of a path and a line integral of a function along that path." :tutorial/id #uuid "56e8bdd3-5303-5eeb-82d3-ef837773c38f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5797 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65361afd-5d8b-59a0-8bed-041764bc0685" :step/math-academy-id 35544 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173202 :tutorial/content "Now suppose that a curve $C$ is defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta), \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2.\n$$\n\nWe've already seen that a polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2.\n$$\n\nUsing the usual formula for the arc length of a polar curve, the arc length of $C$ can be calculated as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as the line integral\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L,$ the arc-length element $\\textrm ds$ for a curve can also be defined using polar coordinates as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll build up a geometric understanding of this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's use this result to calculate some line integrals across polar curves." :tutorial/id #uuid "4850750a-5b8d-5c03-88c1-a2b832a313ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5801 :tutorial/title "The Arc Length Element for Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "665cda4d-3863-540c-afb3-a1cde7ebd6d9" :step/math-academy-id 35565 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173203 :tutorial/content "Let's now build up a geometric understanding of what the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ means. First, we'll consider the case of a parametric curve defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSuppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b302806eef01fd6e8160ee6e3183959537c0e9f5c6907238a2eea1d208d3da7.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta t$ in the parameter $t.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0b1eb1dcc7be637e4e6024b5a8e9e13a4ea5c3e7439be7a2e3083f5ec274ef71.png)\n\nAccording to the diagram we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = x(t+\\Delta t) - x(t), \\qquad \\Delta y = y(t+\\Delta t) - y(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta y$ are the changes in $x$ and $y,$ respectively, corresponding to the change in $t.$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, these quantities can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\Delta x \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t, \\qquad \\Delta y \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t.\n$$\n\nThe change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Therefore, by the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & \\approx ||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & ={(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2} \\\\  & \\approx {(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2} \\\\  & =[{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}](Δt)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s\n\n\\approx\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\Delta t.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta t \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\textrm d t\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65578a76-05f7-551a-acbd-956ae6831965" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5802 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Arc Length Element for a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "46feb8c2-5068-5992-a3a2-858b37d8c39e" :step/math-academy-id 35569 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173204 :tutorial/content "Now, let's derive the formula for the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ for a curve defined using plane polar coordinates. We will use a similar geometric approach.\n\nConsider a curve $r= r(\\theta)$ defined using polar coordinates. Suppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/7f/7fa77b8d4ad159967f917b438096f472cbd7f541495847e11b919a6843e6259b.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta \\theta$ in the parameter $\\theta.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/10/105112a2bb993a13282c5f36190ca99a46d80a22a4203f653f7f67060e486d48.png)\n\nFrom here, we see that the vector $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ can be approximated as the vector addition of two components:\n\n- a vector of length $\\Delta r$ acting parallel to $\\mathbf r,$ and\n\n- a vector of length $r\\Delta \\theta$ acting perpendicular to $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/08/080fa1112410328ba369e1c2e5d70fc2e5ddba97606a2fdeaa90d61e3e20d874.png)\n\nNote that $\\Delta r$ is simply the difference of the radial lengths between the points $P$ and $P'.$ The expression $r\\Delta \\theta$ comes from the formula for the arc length of a sector of a circle.\n\nNow, the change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Since our two components are perpendicular, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & \\approx ||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & \\approx (Δr)^{2}+(rΔ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =Δ{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta r = r(\\theta + \\Delta\\theta) - r(\\theta) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $(\\Delta s)^2$ above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & \\approx {(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =[{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}](Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta \\theta \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\textrm d\\theta\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b310dc84-9da7-5492-9639-145086fe7403" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5803 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Arc Length Element for a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "6cd1a226-a48b-5c1c-9eed-9c80bd2de431" :step/math-academy-id 35570}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172176}]}
